---
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro'
import Container from '@/components/Container.astro'
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro'
import { representingTasteProject } from '@/research/representing-taste'
import { structProject } from '@/research/struct-project'
---

<Layout
  title={representingTasteProject.title}
  description={representingTasteProject.summary}
  canonicalPath={representingTasteProject.path}
>
  <Container class="flex flex-col gap-y-10 pb-24">
    <Breadcrumbs
      items={[
        { label: 'Research', href: '/research/', i18nKey: 'nav.research' },
        { label: 'REPResenting Taste' },
      ]}
    />
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        {representingTasteProject.status}
      </p>
      <h1 class="max-w-4xl text-3xl font-bold">
        {representingTasteProject.title}
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        {representingTasteProject.summary}
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-3 text-sm">
        <a
          class="font-semibold underline underline-offset-4"
          href={structProject.demoPath}>Try the Struct demo →</a
        >
        <a
          class="underline underline-offset-4"
          href={representingTasteProject.sourcePlan}>Research plan</a
        >
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose max-w-[70ch] dark:prose-invert">
      <h2>Problem</h2>
      <p>
        Much of creative production is a grind of adaptation. Content changes.
        Collaborators bring different versions. Work moves between formats and
        audiences. Each change can mean repeating adjustments, reconstructing
        earlier choices, and repairing their knock-on effects.
      </p>
      <p>
        Workflows built around sequential edits make these operations cumbersome
        and repetitive. The judgments behind the work remain difficult to
        isolate, reuse or revise.
      </p>

      <h2>Approach</h2>
      <p>
        Develop software that represents content, creative decisions and their
        dependencies as explicit, editable structures. A decision records what
        it applies to, its scope, the conditions under which it remains valid,
        and its relationships to other decisions. The model is extensible with
        new decision types and operations.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={structProject.demoPath}>Struct</a> supplies the document layer: structured
        content, assets, relationships, and reading renditions. The decision layer
        is application-owned and proposed; it does not make Struct responsible for
        creative policy or revisions. It is not a universal Struct schema or a competing
        canonical editorial store.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <ol
          class="not-prose m-0 grid list-none gap-3 p-0 text-sm md:grid-cols-4"
          aria-label="Proposed REPResenting Taste software architecture"
        >
          <li class="border p-3">
            <a
              class="font-bold underline underline-offset-4"
              href={structProject.demoPath}>Document</a
            >
            <span class="block text-muted-foreground">Content and assets</span>
          </li>
          <li class="border p-3">
            <strong>Decisions</strong>
            <span class="block text-muted-foreground"
              >Scope and dependencies</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="border p-3">
            <strong>Operations</strong>
            <span class="block text-muted-foreground"
              >Replay, edit, fork, remix</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="border p-3">
            <strong>Renditions</strong>
            <span class="block text-muted-foreground"
              >Digital and offline editions</span
            >
          </li>
        </ol>
        <figcaption>
          A bounded, application-owned source of truth for content, creative
          decisions, and dependencies supports parallel branches and forms
          derived for different contexts.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        A typed API would apply operations with preconditions, validity checks,
        and conflict diagnostics. People and agents can then share a state,
        develop variants independently, combine accepted decisions, and check
        what remains applicable after change.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Capability</th>
            <th>Software method</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Replay</td>
            <td>Record operations against a known state and check validity.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Edit</td>
            <td
              >Inspect and change content, decisions, scope, and dependencies.</td
            >
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Parallel</td>
            <td
              >Fork shared state for independent variants and contributions.</td
            >
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Remix</td>
            <td
              >Compose selected decisions and expose interactions and conflicts.</td
            >
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h3>Proposed evaluation</h3>
      <p>
        Matched tasks will compare this workflow with a strong native workflow
        using the same content briefs, model access, and renderer. The primary
        measure is total active editing time: capture, composition, adaptation,
        checking, and repair. The proposed initial target is at least 20% less
        active work, with retained decisions, completion, and blind output
        quality as secondary measures. A five-task pilot precedes a
        counterbalanced editor study.
      </p>

      <h2>Why Now</h2>
      <p>
        Replay, branching, and remixing existed before large language models.
        Autonomous creative work now needs explicit shared state, accepted
        decisions, and dependencies so people and agents can inspect, combine,
        and check changes. REPResenting Taste investigates those foundations.
      </p>

      <h2>Outcome</h2>
      <p>
        An AI-native magazine, as one application of the representation and its
        operations, that can be created and revised collaboratively, then
        published across digital and offline formats.
      </p>
      <h3>Milestones</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Running:</strong> Struct’s <a href={structProject.demoPath}
          >PDF-import and responsive-reading demo</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Planned:</strong> a shared canonical representation for content, creative
        decisions, and dependencies across forms; a five-task pilot; a complete issue
        with Margin; held-out editor and reader studies; then a REPResenting Taste
        research-paper submission.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Container>
</Layout>
